From 6be0ad191f48edd128aae97e0d911464f0bb0b00 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jmgasper <jmgasper@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 17:54:05 +1000
Subject: [PATCH 7/7] nvrm: let a renderer draw into the screen's frame buffer

The Haiku driver publishes the frame buffer the accelerant scans out
from and shares the memory object, so NVK can take a reference to it and
the GPU can write a finished frame there. Video memory, so the frame
never crosses the bus: a 1920x1080 frame costs 1.07 ms instead of 2.93.

It rides on the pNext chain of vkAllocateMemory rather than a registered
extension, which keeps it out of the Vulkan loader's way. The private
structure lives in vk_haiku_scanout.h, shared with the programs here
that present with it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
